Z||000000||GT Bastard||02-23-2000||10:43 PM||||Rebuild an alternator!?  My Gt's alternator is dying and I'm ready to either replace or rebuild it. Is it really that tough?  I can rebuild a toaster, so I'm pretty decent at mechanical stuff. ||||reg||14
Z||000001||Phaeton||02-23-2000||11:17 PM||||If all the neccessary parts are available it is pretty straightforward, but if no press is handy the bearings will have to be beat out and in. Be anal on the alignment before smacking them and everything should be fine. The armature I spun with compressed air and used a file rather than sandpaper, I was told that grit would eat the brushes if sandpaper was used, I doubt it but took no chances anyway.  The rest is just R & R of parts.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Tom88gt||02-24-2000||09:26 AM||||Why rebuild?  Most GM type alternators can be purchased at an auto parts store for $19.95/$29.95  Is the Fireo some kind of wierd expensive one?  Anyway, a kit costs about $10-15 and is pretty easy to install.  I had to use one in Greece.  But I skipped the bearing because I didn't have a press and was lazy.  But it's almost always the diodes that go bad, not the bearing.||||reg||5
Z||000003||lowCG||02-25-2000||06:59 PM||||My Lancia had a GM alternator in it,and I used to toast the front bearing every couple of weeks after buzzing to 9,000RPM.It wasn't too tough,the bearing cost about $10.<BR>You'll probably need an impact wrench for the pulley nut.||||reg||1
